For background runs, force unbuffered output so logs appear in real time
(`python -u`, or `PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1`, or the equivalent for your language):

```bash
bash -c "
cd [SET AT SETUP: PROJECT_WORKING_ROOT]
PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 [SET AT SETUP: PACKAGE_MANAGER_RUN_CMD] your_script.py [args]
" > /path/to/log 2>&1 &
```

Always force unbuffered mode explicitly — know your tool's buffering
behavior before trusting a live log tail.

## Detached launches (rule 15)

A bare `cmd > log 2>&1 &` is still a child of the launching shell — if that
shell dies (terminal/session crash, SSH drop), the whole process group can
get SIGHUP and every un-detached job dies with it, silently: no traceback,
no OOM line, no watchdog alert. If your session runs inside a
terminal-multiplexer-managed cgroup (e.g. tmux under a systemd user
session), a torn-down scope can SIGKILL every process in that cgroup
regardless of session leadership — plain `setsid`/`disown` is not enough by
itself in that case.

`[SET AT SETUP: LAUNCH_METHOD]` selects which pattern below applies to this
project (set during setup based on what's actually available on the target
machine — a systemd user manager, a container, or neither). The other two
patterns still exist and are fully documented in
`.friday/harness/rules/environment.md.tmpl` — worth a look if this project's
launch method ever changes.
<!-- SECTION:launch_systemd:start -->
**`LAUNCH_METHOD=systemd-run-user`** — this machine has a user systemd
manager available; always use it, since work here is launched over SSH/tmux
and a dropped connection or closed tmux pane must not kill a running job:

```bash
systemd-run --user --scope --unit=<unique-descriptive-name> -- bash -c "
cd [SET AT SETUP: PROJECT_WORKING_ROOT]
PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 [SET AT SETUP: PACKAGE_MANAGER_RUN_CMD] your_script.py [args]
" > harness/running/logs/your_script.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &
disown
```

Verify with the process's cgroup path: it must NOT contain the launching
session's own scope component.
<!-- SECTION:launch_systemd:end -->
<!-- SECTION:launch_setsid:start -->
**`LAUNCH_METHOD=setsid-nohup`** (no user systemd manager available) — full
detachment via `setsid`/`nohup`:

```bash
setsid nohup bash -c "
[env setup]
cd [repo root]
[unbuffered run command] your_script [args]
" > harness/running/logs/your_script.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &
disown
```

`setsid` puts the process in a new session (immune to the parent terminal's
SIGHUP); `nohup` is belt-and-suspenders; `< /dev/null` avoids a hung read on
closed stdin; `disown` drops it from the launching shell's job table.
Verify with a process inspection command that session-id equals pid. This
protects against SIGHUP but NOT against a cgroup-wide kill — prefer the
service-manager pattern above wherever available.
<!-- SECTION:launch_setsid:end -->
<!-- SECTION:launch_container:start -->
**`LAUNCH_METHOD=setsid-nohup-container`** (running inside the project's
Docker container) — use the same `setsid`/`nohup` pattern as above; no user
systemd manager exists inside a minimal container image, and running
systemd *inside* Docker is unnecessary complexity here. `docker-compose.yml`
runs the container with `init: true` (Compose's built-in `tini` as PID 1),
which reaps zombie processes from detached jobs the same way a service
manager would on bare metal — that's what keeps this pattern safe across
many detached launches in one long-lived container.
<!-- SECTION:launch_container:end -->

<!-- SECTION:batch_hardening:start -->
### Batch job memory hardening

[SET AT SETUP: delete this section if not applicable — per-item batch jobs
can have wildly variable per-item memory footprint that doesn't track any
obvious size proxy. Mitigate with worker recycling (so one bad item doesn't
permanently bloat a long-lived worker), per-item process isolation (so one
OOM kills one item, not the batch), and resource-aware dispatch throttling
(so the job self-paces under memory pressure).]
<!-- SECTION:batch_hardening:end -->

<!-- SECTION:job_overlap:start -->
### Verify a prior job has fully exited before launching the next

[SET AT SETUP: delete this section if the project has no shared-device/
shared-resource concern — two jobs can briefly overlap on one device, halving
available memory and causing a false resource-exhaustion crash. Before the
real launch, confirm the prior job's process/service is fully STOPPED and
the device shows its memory freed. A bare process-ID check is not enough if
your launcher may still be tearing down child processes.]
<!-- SECTION:job_overlap:end -->

## Dependencies

Declared in `[SET AT SETUP: DEPENDENCY_MANIFEST]` at the repo root, locked
via `[SET AT SETUP: LOCKFILE]`. Add a new dependency with
`[SET AT SETUP: PACKAGE_MANAGER_ADD_CMD] <package>` (never hand-edit the
lock file).

All scripts use paths relative to the repo root
(`[SET AT SETUP: PROJECT_WORKING_ROOT]`); no hardcoded absolute paths.

## Key script commands

```bash
[SET AT SETUP: PACKAGE_MANAGER_SYNC_CMD]                         # install/update env
[SET AT SETUP: TEST_CMD]                                         # run tests
git commit                                                        # see harness/rules/version_control.md — Reviewer only, role-prefixed
```

<!-- SECTION:multi_machine:start -->
## Multi-machine work

[SET AT SETUP: delete this section if the project never partitions work
across multiple machines — when partitioning work across machines, inputs
assigned to one machine MUST be physically **moved** out of (or
hard-excluded from) every other machine's input pool **before any parallel
launch**:

- Never use iteration-order timing as an implicit partition ("machine A
  works upward, so machine B's tail is safe for N hours"). A script's real
  sort order may not be the one you assumed — lexicographic vs. numeric is
  the classic trap.
- If every machine still sees the full pool, no machine can know the others
  exist; duplicated work goes undetected, and if the job is stochastic the
  duplicates aren't even byte-identical.
- Separate each machine's inputs into its own directory, confirm zero
  overlap with a set-difference check, then restart any already-running
  process — one that loaded its input list at startup won't notice an
  on-disk move.]
<!-- SECTION:multi_machine:end -->
